      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} - x - 1$ at $\\left(1,-4\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- 2 x^{2} - x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=- 4 \\left(1\\right) - 1=-5\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-5\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-4\\right)&=\\frac{1}{5}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{5}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{5} - \\frac{21}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $b=- \\frac{21}{5}$."
